x":[-54,40,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-54,40,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-49,40,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-49,40,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-44,40,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-44,40,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-39,40,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-39,40,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-66,50,0,30,36,10],"m":"ochre"},{"gable":[-66,50,10,30,36,2.2],"m":"tile","axis":"y"},{"box":[-64,86,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-64,86,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-59,86,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-59,86,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-54,86,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-54,86,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-49,86,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-49,86,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-44,86,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-44,86,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-39,86,3,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"},{"box":[-39,86,6.5,1.4,0.4,2.4],"m":"dark"}],"notes":"The Gallerie dell'Accademia in Venice: the church of Santa Maria della Carità, the Scuola Grande della Carità and the Lateran canons' convent with Palladio's cloister, on the Campo della Carità at the foot of the Accademia bridge. Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions and the OpenStreetMap outlines in the ground file, not photographs. The ground puts the museum's point on the campo and the four-storey complex south of it (about 60 by 92 m, a ring round an inner court), the Grand Canal to the north-east; the old model stood some 30 m north-west of the outline, so it was rebuilt on the outline, square to the grid as the outline is. Now: on the campo, the church's Gothic brick front with its gable, pointed portal in a stone frame, round window and buttresses, and beside it Massari's white Istrian-stone front of the Scuola with pilasters, entablature and pediment, its doorway and windows; the church nave running south under a tile roof with its tall flank windows; the convent ranges in rose render under tile roofs round Palladio's cloister (brick arcades on two sides, the brick elevation above); the long east range with the galleries' skylight; rows of windows on the outer fronts; the canal, the fondamenta and the wooden bridge rising off the campo; low neighbouring houses either side. Still guessed: every height, the court's size, the window rhythm, which range is which, the skylight's extent, and the bridge's line beyond the site."}